:name: SX1262, with no firmware in the way
:description: Drives the Renode peripheral directly, before any firmware depends on it.
:
: What is under test is the seam between the peripheral and the chip library it
: loads: chip select framing, byte order, and that the answers coming back are
: the ones a native node gets from the same model.
:
: No engine and no CPU. A machine with neither is deliberate: what is being
: asked is whether the peripheral and the model agree, and a firmware would only
: add its own opinion to a question that does not need one.
:
:   MESHBENCH_RADIO_LIB=/path/to/libvirtualsx1262.so \
:     renode tools/renode/peripherals/radio-only.resc

mach create "radio"
i @tools/renode/peripherals/VirtualSX1262Lib.cs
i @tools/renode/peripherals/VirtualSX1262Engine.cs
i @tools/renode/peripherals/VirtualSX1262.cs
machine LoadPlatformDescription @tools/renode/peripherals/radio-only.repl

radio Connect

: GetStatus (0xC0) then one byte clocked out to read the answer. A chip in
: standby answers 0x20 - mode 2 in bits 6:4 - and a chip that is not there
: answers 0x00, which is how "no chip model" looks from the firmware's side.
echo "GetStatus, expect 0x2x:"
radio Transmit 0xC0
radio Transmit 0x00

: WriteBuffer at offset 0, three bytes, then read them back. This is the one
: that caught the earlier model out: the buffer commands are variable length and
: run until chip select deasserts, so a model treating them as fixed length
: parses payload as opcodes and returns garbage after the first byte.
echo "WriteBuffer AA BB CC:"
radio Transmit 0x0E
radio Transmit 0x00
radio Transmit 0xAA
radio Transmit 0xBB
radio Transmit 0xCC
radio FinishTransmission

echo "ReadBuffer, expect AA BB CC:"
radio Transmit 0x1E
radio Transmit 0x00
radio Transmit 0x00
radio Transmit 0x00
radio Transmit 0x00
radio Transmit 0x00
radio FinishTransmission
